In JavaScript, when you use `var` to declare a variable, it is scoped to that function or block. This means that any changes made inside the scope of `var` will be visible outside of it. 

For example:

```javascript
for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
  console.log(i);
}
```

When you run this code, it prints the number 0, then 1, and so on, because `i` is initialized to 0 within the scope of the `for` loop. However, if you change the initial value of `i` to 5, the output will still be 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, because the `for` loop has access to the global object (`window` in modern browsers), which contains all variables defined before the `for` loop.
